sfied\",\"summary\":\"A dedicated /etc/logrotate.d/inventory-app config was created with 'daily' directive and validated via logrotate -d dry-run without errors, confirming correct syntax and scheduling logic against the active daily logrotate.timer.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f39e5e429aa84790\",\"cmd-f76b5e89862c4fd5\",\"cmd-4600339b90534551\"]},{\"id\":\"seven-compressed-rotations\",\"requirement\":\"Keep seven compressed rotations\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Config specifies rotate 7, compress, delaycompress. A 9-cycle forced rotation test directly demonstrated the retention cap holds at exactly 7 rotations (app.log.1 uncompressed per delaycompress, app.log.2.gz through app.log.7.gz compressed), with no older files retained.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f39e5e429aa84790\",\"cmd-59cc06c4193348fb\"]},{\"id\":\"continued-writing\",\"requirement\":\"Allow the application to continue writing to the log after rotation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"copytruncate was used; a forced rotation preserved the original inode and a post-rotation write test succeeded on the same inode, confirming continued writability after rotation. This behavior was re-confirmed after the node restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-81ae2ddbcc854342\",\"cmd-3c067dcb97a64b1d\",\"cmd-db7de8c27ab64e2d\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"The executor's mutating commands were all directly tied to verifying the material outcomes (forced rotation cycles, inode/write checks, the mandated restart) or were bounded cleanup of self-created test artifacts. cmd-81ae2ddbcc854342, cmd-3c067dcb97a64b1d, cmd-59cc06c4193348fb, and the post-restart force-rotate/write in cmd-db7de8c27ab64e2d created rotation test files and status-file entries, but these were fully reverted by cmd-36ec42b0f978482a and cmd-0c3bcb3cdc6142ff, restoring app.log content and /var/lib/logrotate/status to their pre-test state (matching the original inspected content in cmd-4600339b90534551). The only unreverted minor residue is a temp diagnostic file /tmp/predr.log written by cmd-86264a9327304cd2 (logrotate -d dry-run output redirected there) that was never removed; this is low-risk, non-managed-state residue with no functional impact. No failed services, no unsafe exposure changes, and package/dpkg health remained clean per the after_executor snapshot. Socket/port and boot-identity differences in the executor comparison are explained entirely by the required node restart, not unrelated mutation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-36ec42b0f978482a\",\"cmd-0c3bcb3cdc6142ff\",\"cmd-86264a9327304cd2\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task — daily rotation, a 7-rotation compressed retention cap, and continued writability after rotation via copytruncate — are directly and concretely demonstrated through forced rotation cycles and inode/write checks, not just inferred from configuration. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also executed cleanly: pre-restart baseline, a reboot whose acknowledgement returned before disconnect, a directly observed boot_id change (dfeb3b70... to 79de688f..., matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and post-restart re-verification that the config, timer, and rotation/write behavior all recovered. Test artifacts were cleaned up after each verification phase, leaving the log content matching its pre-task state.\",\"limitations\":[\"A minor temp file /tmp/predr.log created during the pre-restart baseline capture was never cleaned up; low-risk but not fully restored.\",\"No independent evidence from a real application process was captured showing a live writer surviving copytruncate, since no owning process for app.log was found on the node; the inode-preservation and manual write test are used as the practical proxy for 'continue writing'.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"daily-rotation","requirement":"Rotate /var/log/inventory/app.log daily","status":"satisfied","summary":"A dedicated /etc/logrotate.d/inventory-app config was created with 'daily' directive and validated via logrotate -d dry-run without errors, confirming correct syntax and scheduling logic against the active daily logrotate.timer.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f39e5e429aa84790","cmd-f76b5e89862c4fd5","cmd-4600339b90534551"]},{"id":"seven-compressed-rotations","requirement":"Keep seven compressed rotations","status":"satisfied","summary":"Config specifies rotate 7, compress, delaycompress. A 9-cycle forced rotation test directly demonstrated the retention cap holds at exactly 7 rotations (app.log.1 uncompressed per delaycompress, app.log.2.gz through app.log.7.gz compressed), with no older files retained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f39e5e429aa84790","cmd-59cc06c4193348fb"]},{"id":"continued-writing","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing to the log after rotation","status":"satisfied","summary":"copytruncate was used; a forced rotation preserved the original inode and a post-rotation write test succeeded on the same inode, confirming continued writability after rotation. This behavior was re-confirmed after the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-81ae2ddbcc854342","cmd-3c067dcb97a64b1d","cmd-db7de8c27ab64e2d"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The executor's mutating commands were all directly tied to verifying the material outcomes (forced rotation cycles, inode/write checks, the mandated restart) or were bounded cleanup of self-created test artifacts. cmd-81ae2ddbcc854342, cmd-3c067dcb97a64b1d, cmd-59cc06c4193348fb, and the post-restart force-rotate/write in cmd-db7de8c27ab64e2d created rotation test files and status-file entries, but these were fully reverted by cmd-36ec42b0f978482a and cmd-0c3bcb3cdc6142ff, restoring app.log content and /var/lib/logrotate/status to their pre-test state (matching the original inspected content in cmd-4600339b90534551). The only unreverted minor residue is a temp diagnostic file /tmp/predr.log written by cmd-86264a9327304cd2 (logrotate -d dry-run output redirected there) that was never removed; this is low-risk, non-managed-state residue with no functional impact. No failed services, no unsafe exposure changes, and package/dpkg health remained clean per the after_executor snapshot. Socket/port and boot-identity differences in the executor comparison are explained entirely by the required node restart, not unrelated mutation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-36ec42b0f978482a","cmd-0c3bcb3cdc6142ff","cmd-86264a9327304cd2","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task — daily rotation, a 7-rotation compressed retention cap, and continued writability after rotation via copytruncate — are directly and concretely demonstrated through forced rotation cycles and inode/write checks, not just inferred from configuration. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also executed cleanly: pre-restart baseline, a reboot whose acknowledgement returned before disconnect, a directly observed boot_id change (dfeb3b70... to 79de688f..., matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and post-restart re-verification that the config, timer, and rotation/write behavior all recovered. Test artifacts were cleaned up after each verification phase, leaving the log content matching its pre-task state.","limitations":["A minor temp file /tmp/predr.log created during the pre-restart baseline capture was never cleaned up; low-risk but not fully restored.","No independent evidence from a real application process was captured showing a live writer surviving copytruncate, since no owning process for app.log was found on the node; the inode-preservation and manual write test are used as the practical proxy for 'continue writing'."]},"ttft_ms":28214,"type":"result","duration_ms":54942,"uuid":"fff44693-3de3-466f